INT. TIDAL POWER STATION - OCEAN PLANET HABITAT - DAY

A glass-walled control gallery above a roaring turbine bay. Seawater thunders through the lower deck. Everything hums. Everything is calm.

YSOLDE FARAH (40s, station chief, dark braid, mug of tea) sits at the main console and watches a wall of green graphs. PAVAN IYER (30s, technician, sleeves rolled, a pen behind each ear) moves between panels, tapping readouts and writing nothing down. MR. GREAVES (60s, inspector from the Ministry, gray suit, a slim case) stands at the window with his back to them.

The graphs scroll. All green.

YSOLDE
You'll find we're ahead of schedule, Mr. Greaves.

GREAVES
So I've heard.

PAVAN
Three weeks ahead. Four, if you count the sluice.

GREAVES
I don't count the sluice.

PAVAN
Then three.

Ysolde sips her tea. She sets it down, slightly off the coaster, and nudges it back with one finger. Pavan stops tapping. For a moment the only sound is the sea in the bay below.

Greaves turns from the window and lays his slim case on the console. He opens it. Inside, a thin tablet. He taps it, and a schedule opens, yellow with warnings. Ysolde leans forward to read it, and Pavan leans forward to read it over her shoulder, and Greaves watches them both without a flicker of expression.

YSOLDE
That's the old log.

GREAVES
That's the log you filed.

PAVAN
(carefully)
It must be a sync issue.

GREAVES
I don't think so.

Pavan wipes his palms on his trousers. Ysolde takes the pen from behind his ear, and puts it in her pocket, and closes the case, gently, as one closes the eyes of the dead.

YSOLDE
Pavan, would you get Mr. Greaves some tea?

PAVAN
Of course.

He doesn't move.

GREAVES
He has already seen the turbines, Ms. Farah.

YSOLDE
Then you know what we've done.

GREAVES
I know what you haven't.

Below, the roar of the water changes pitch. A slow, rising note, like a held breath. The graphs on the wall go amber, then red, one after another down the row.

Nobody looks at them. Ysolde picks up her tea. Her hand is perfectly steady.

YSOLDE
How long?

GREAVES
Four minutes.

PAVAN
For what?

Greaves walks to the door. He holds it open for the two of them, politely, the way a man holds a door for guests leaving a very good party.
